WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.640
A clean empty-frame layout can become unserviceable when populated.

2
00:00:04.019 --> 00:00:08.380
Cables and hoses occupy volume, need support and move during installation or

3
00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:08.920
replacement.

4
00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:13.340
Their permitted routing and bend conditions belong in the configuration review, not

5
00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:14.820
in an improvised field arrangement.

6
00:00:15.340 --> 00:00:18.860
The I T Gear Design Guide includes access and keep-out guidance around

7
00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:20.360
blind-mate equipment and tubing.

8
00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:24.240
Use the relevant drawing and supplier limits to check the actual layout.

9
00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:28.120
Reserve the tool and hand access required by the approved service method.

10
00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:31.540
Don't route a new bundle through an envelope needed to remove another

11
00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:31.820
item.

12
00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:36.280
Review the densest permitted configuration and the service movement, not only the

13
00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:37.320
final resting position.

14
00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:41.340
Check that strain isn't transferred into a coupling or electrical connection when

15
00:00:41.380 --> 00:00:42.660
adjacent equipment moves.

16
00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:46.100
Then identify who owns changes after deployment.

17
00:00:46.520 --> 00:00:50.700
A routing improvement for one subsystem can undermine another subsystem's access or

18
00:00:50.740 --> 00:00:51.140
airflow.

19
00:00:51.620 --> 00:00:56.060
Serviceability is therefore a shared interface requirement, not a cosmetic preference to

20
00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:58.700
consider after all capacity decisions are complete.

